\addcontentsline{toc}{chapter}{\bf ABSTRACT}

\begin{center}
    \large{\bf ABSTRACT}
\end{center}

    The rising demand for mental health support has accelerated the development of AI-driven psychological counseling. However, most current AIs rely solely on textual information, lacking the ability to comprehend nuanced human emotions. While prior research indicates a correlation between vocal cues and depression, a framework for leveraging these cues for real-time therapeutic intervention during conversation is still missing. This research introduces a novel framework for an Emotionally Intelligent AI that integrates paralinguistic vocal cue analysis (e.g., pitch, loudness, duration) with Cognitive Behavioral Therapy (CBT) dialogues to detect negative cognitive states and deliver immediate, appropriate micro-interventions. We hypothesize that this model will achieve higher accuracy in detecting complex cognitive states, such as rumination or cognitive distortion, compared to text-only models. This work represents a significant step towards creating more human-like AI counselors by bridging the gap between passive emotional detection and proactive therapeutic assistance.

